From 2f72dba7e8081f57b6064537dc121a2381b9454b Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Alex Kudentsov <43482574+alexk37@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Fri, 16 Oct 2026 23:20:21 +0700
Subject: [PATCH 36/37] linux-cp: T9481: do not drain on pair add while the
 netlink socket is closed

lcp_nl_pair_add_cb() calls lcp_nl_drain_messages() on every LCP pair add
made outside netlink processing. During a resynchronization
nl_route_process() closes the notification socket (lcp_nl_close_socket(),
sk_route = NULL) and suspends for sync_attempt_delay_ms before it opens
the socket again. A pair add in that window makes lcp_nl_drain_messages()
return -NLE_BAD_SOCK, which it treats as a read error and signals
NL_EVENT_ERR without logging it. The process node is suspended, so the
event stays pending and is returned by the first
vlib_process_wait_for_event_or_clock() after the link dump. The round
then jumps to sync_later: no address, neighbor or route dump, the newly
opened notification socket is closed again about 1 ms after it was
opened, and every kernel notification sent until the next round is lost.

On VyOS a bond rebuild re-creates the bond's LCP pair inside that window,
so the first round after a netlink overflow always aborted, and a VLAN
that came up after the first link dump lost its UP notification.

Return -NLE_BAD_SOCK from lcp_nl_drain_messages() without signalling
NL_EVENT_ERR when sk_route is NULL. There is nothing to drain without a
socket, and errors on an open socket still signal NL_EVENT_ERR as before.
The pair add callback is the only caller that can reach a closed socket:
nl_route_read_cb() runs only while the socket is polled, and
lcp_nl_close_socket() stops polling before it frees the socket.
---
 src/plugins/linux-cp/lcp_nl.c | 11 +++++++++--
 1 file changed, 9 insertions(+), 2 deletions(-)

diff --git a/src/plugins/linux-cp/lcp_nl.c b/src/plugins/linux-cp/lcp_nl.c
index da8edd48a..c181a6461 100644
--- a/src/plugins/linux-cp/lcp_nl.c
+++ b/src/plugins/linux-cp/lcp_nl.c
@@ -788,9 +788,16 @@ lcp_nl_drain_messages (void)
   int err;
   nl_main_t *nm = &nl_main;
 
+  /* The notification socket is closed while a synchronization waits for
+   * its next attempt. There is nothing to drain then. Do not signal
+   * NL_EVENT_ERR: the process node is suspended and would only see the
+   * event after the link dump, aborting that synchronization.
+   */
+  if (!nm->sk_route)
+    return -NLE_BAD_SOCK;
+
   /* Read until there's an error */
-  while ((err = nm->sk_route ? nl_recvmsgs_default (nm->sk_route) :
-			       -NLE_BAD_SOCK) > -1)
+  while ((err = nl_recvmsgs_default (nm->sk_route)) > -1)
     ;
 
   /* If there was an error other then EAGAIN, signal process node */
